def E(date_iso,date_display,precision,eye,place,headline,digest,conf,src,quote=""):
    return dict(date_iso=date_iso,date_display=date_display,precision=precision,eye=eye,place=place,headline=headline,digest=digest,quote=quote,confidence=conf,source_note=src)

entries=[
E("1720-03-06","6 March 1720","day","virmondt","Istanbul","The French ambassador is behind the choice of envoy",
 "Virmondt told Vienna that the Marquis de Bonnac had steered the Porte toward Mehmed Efendi as envoy. He read the mission as a way to probe the Habsburg–French alliance and to gather news of northern affairs, with the real instructions given by word of mouth.","verified",BEL),
E("1720-03-13","2/13 March 1720 (O.S./N.S.)","day","stanyan","Pera, Istanbul","Embassy said to be shelved over its cost",
 "Stanyan reported that the planned mission to France had been suspended because of the expense and that perhaps nobody would be sent after all. The British in Istanbul still saw it as a doubtful, budget-driven idea.","verified",BEL+" (SP 97/24)"),
E("1720-04-16","16 April 1720","day","virmondt","Istanbul","The Grand Vizier offers to show the sultan's letter",
 "İbrahim Pasha told Virmondt that the envoy would carry an imperial letter to Paris and offered to let him see it. Virmondt kept a studied indifference so as not to seem alarmed by the French connection.","verified",BEL),
E("1720-05-17","6/17 May 1720 (O.S./N.S.)","day","stanyan","Pera, Istanbul","Suspicion of a Franco-Ottoman treaty",
 "Stanyan noted frequent meetings between Mehmed and the French dragoman Le Noir and suspected a turn toward a treaty with France, encouraged by Bonnac. The appointment, he had heard, was confirmed but kept quiet until the Habsburg envoy left.","verified",BEL+" (SP 97/24)"),
E("1720-08-17","12 Şevval 1132 AH (17 August 1720)","day","mehmed","Istanbul","Promoted chief treasurer before the journey",
 "Mehmed was given the rank of first treasurer (şıkk-ı evvel defterdarı), raising his standing for the mission; the formal appointment as ambassador had come a week earlier. The imperial letter he would carry was drafted in mid-Zilkade 1132 (mid-September 1720).","verified",BEL+" (Hijri date from its footnote)"),
E("1720-10","8 and 19 October 1720","range","dirling","Istanbul","A bribed insider lists the embassy's aims",
 "Dirling's informant in the ambassador's household claimed the mission had three goals, one of them reviving the cause of the Hungarian exile Rákóczi. The Habsburg agent doubted that the embassy was only about delivering a letter; the Porte's interpreter denied any alliance plan.","approx",BEL+"; which of the October reports carries which detail is not specified"),
E("1720-10-07","4 Zilhicce 1132 AH (7 October 1720)","day","mehmed","Istanbul","Embarking on a French ship for France",
 "Mehmed went aboard a French vessel with his suite, among them his son Said, and opened his narrative on the day of departure. The fleet paused three days at the Dardanelles before sailing on 14 October.","verified",TH1),
E("1720-10-14","14 October 1720","day","penterriedter","Paris","Dubois would like to stop the Turk",
 "Pentenriedter reported that Dubois wanted to delay or block the embassy, perhaps by holding the envoy at Toulon, giving him presents and sending him home. The French were short of money after the Law crash and frightened by plague in Provence.","verified",BEL),
E("1720-10-13","13 October 1720","day","sutton","Paris","Dubois plans to halt the envoy at Toulon",
 "Sutton told London that Dubois meant to stop the Ottoman envoy at Toulon, and suggested Bonnac had arranged the mission for his own credit. The British ambassador treated it as a French embarrassment rather than a threat.","approx",BEL+" (SP 78/169; Old/New Style not stated)"),
E("1720-10-25","25 October 1720","day","roquelaure","Montpellier","Maguelone chosen for a second quarantine",
 "With no news yet of the ambassador's landing, Roquelaure proposed that the visitors undergo a second quarantine in Languedoc, and the deserted island church of Maguelone was officially chosen that day.","verified",TH2),
E("1720-11-04","4 November 1720","day","dubois","Paris","Hoping the embassy might not come",
 "Dubois wrote to Bonnac in Constantinople that no word had come of the envoy's departure, implying that a cancelled mission would suit France. The order to halt it reached Istanbul only in March 1721, long after Mehmed had sailed.","verified",TH1+"; "+TH2),
E("1720-11-11","11 November 1720","day","bernage","Montpellier","Cotton bales to be burned or aired",
 "Bernage ruled that cotton bales travelling with the embassy must be burned or aired for forty days. In plague year the visitors' baggage was a hazard before it was a gift.","verified",TH2),
E("1720-11-22","21 Muharrem 1133 AH (22 November 1720)","day","mehmed","Toulon roads","Saluted at Toulon but kept aboard",
 "After about forty-five days at sea the ships anchored off Toulon's quarantine hospital to cannon salutes. Mehmed spent the night aboard; he records being told of the quarantine rules only on arrival and describes himself cut off by the plague.","verified",TH2),
E("1720-11-23","23 November 1720","day","mehmed","Toulon, Jardin du Roi (Mourillon)","First steps on French soil, on horseback",
 "Mehmed rode ashore between lines of soldiers, greeted by the intendant with marine officers and music, and was lodged for quarantine in the king's garden at Mourillon until 10 December. He weighed the overland route, judged it too great a burden for his hosts, and agreed to go on by sea.","approx",TH1+"; "+GH),
E("1720-12-16","15 Safer 1133 AH (16 December 1720)","day","mehmed","Sète (Cette)","Reaching Sète after contrary winds",
 "Delayed by winds at Port-de-Bouc (four days in his account, three in the French), the fleet reached Sète in mid-afternoon. The intendant's deputy received him, and the next morning cannon fired as the party set out for Maguelone.","verified",TH1+"; "+TH2),
E("1720-12-17","17 December 1720","day","mehmed","Maguelone","Exiled to a ruined church on an island",
 "Mehmed found himself lodged in the ruined cathedral of Maguelone, cut off from the mainland, and complained of the rooms; he had never expected, he wrote, to be sent into exile. He resolved to bear the forty days with patience, in a proverb about kissing the hem of patience's robe.","approx",TH2+"; "+ARAMCO),
E("1720-12-22","22 December 1720","day","roquelaure","Montpellier","The king's escort officer has arrived",
 "Roquelaure confirmed that de la Baune, the gentleman sent from Paris to conduct the ambassador, had reached Montpellier the evening before. He also counted thirteen French and English travellers who had attached themselves to the embassy at Toulon.","verified",TH2),
E("1721-01-12","12 Rebiülevvel 1133 AH (night of 11/12 January 1721)","day","bernage","Maguelone","Candles for a night of prayer",
 "For the night marking the Prophet's birth and death on 12 Rebiülevvel, Bernage had about 150 candles supplied to the quarantined embassy. It is a rare host record of Muslim worship on French soil, entered as a supply item.","approx",TH2+"; Hijri equivalence computed (tabular calendar, ±1 day)"),
E("1721-01-24","24 January 1721","day","bernage","Montpellier","Sweetmeats, not wine, as gifts",
 "Bernage specified that the towns should honour the ambassador with confections rather than wine. Toulouse's council had already met on 21 January to decide its gifts and name commissioners.","verified",TH1),
E("1721-01-25","25 January 1721","day","dubois","Paris","Separating the hangers-on at quarantine's end",
 "As the quarantine ended, Dubois wrote hoping the foreigners who had joined the embassy had been kept apart and that their costs had been avoided. The minister's eye stayed on the bill.","verified",TH2),
E("1721-01-26","Sunday 26 January 1721","day","mehmed","Frontignan to Agde","Boats on the Canal du Midi",
 "After a reception at Frontignan the day before, the party embarked and reached Agde by late afternoon; on the 27th they were shown Riquet's canal by boat. Mehmed described the canal as a river made from several others for merchants and travellers, and passed Béziers, Le Somail and Trèbes on the way.","approx",TH1+"; quoted in summaries of Grenet (2019)"),
E("1721-02-01","Sunday 1 February 1721","day","mehmed","Toulouse, Port Saint-Étienne","Toulouse: large but somewhat ruined",
 "He left his canal boat at Toulouse in the early afternoon and was taken by carriage to the Hôtel de Pierre. He judged the city very large but rather run down, noted that it was exempt from a royal garrison, and recorded that the fifty soldiers who had escorted him from Toulon took their leave at its gates.","verified",TH1),
E("1721-02-28","28 February 1721","day","roquelaure","Montpellier","A relation of the passage through Languedoc",
 "Roquelaure sent Paris his relation of the embassy's arrival at Sète and its progress through the province. It records the ceremonial, the escorts and the expense of the Languedoc stage, now finished.","verified",TH1+" (AMAE 134CP/11)"),
E("1721-02","February 1721","month","mehmed","Bordeaux","Three days at Bordeaux",
 "From Toulouse the embassy went down the Garonne to Bordeaux, where it spent three days before taking the land road north. Mehmed noted the soldiers drawn up and the notables bringing gifts at each city.","approx",ARAMCO+"; "+GH),
E("1721-03-08","9 Cemaziyelevvel 1133 AH (8 March 1721)","day","mehmed","Charenton and the Faubourg Saint-Antoine","At the gates of Paris, rehearsing the entry",
 "The embassy reached the edge of Paris and waited about a week in the Saint-Antoine quarter. Mehmed met the introducer of ambassadors, Breteuil, several times at Charenton to settle the ceremonial of his entry.","approx",BEL+"; "+ARAMCO+"; "+GH),
E("1721-03-16","Sunday 16 March 1721","day","mercure","Paris","The paper describes the Turkish procession",
 "The Nouveau Mercure described the ceremonial entry: about forty Turks dressed in different colours, sixteen of them carrying pikes topped with horse tails, among French troops and the king's horses. Its interest was in colour, order and costume.","approx",ARAMCO+" (issue describing the entry; day assigned from the entry date)"),
E("1721-03-16","16 March 1721","day","mehmed","Paris, Porte Saint-Antoine to the Hôtel des Ambassadeurs","Riding into Paris on the king's horses",
 "Mehmed entered the capital on horseback, mounted from the royal stables, through streets packed with spectators. He lodged at the Hôtel des Ambassadeurs extraordinaires, where crowds would later come to watch him eat.","verified",GH+"; "+ARAMCO),
E("1721-03-17","17 March 1721","day","penterriedter","Paris","A capable man whose aims need watching",
 "Pentenriedter reported two meetings with the newly arrived envoy, who said he came only to congratulate Louis XV on his accession, as an earlier embassy had honoured the emperor. Pentenriedter found him very able and warned that his real intentions should still be watched.","verified",BEL),
E("1721-03-21","22 Cemaziyelevvel 1133 AH (Friday 21 March 1721)","day","mehmed","Tuileries Palace","Audience with the eleven-year-old king",
 "Mehmed rode to the Tuileries, with his son Said carrying the sultan's letter, and was received by Louis XV before the Regent and the court. Protocol had been bargained in advance: the king stood, and the ambassador was to speak first. He set down the ceremonial of the audience in detail.","verified",VERS+"; "+GH),
E("1721-03-27","27 March 1721","day","mehmed","Paris, Opéra (Palais-Royal)","Thésée at the Opéra: candles and machines",
 "At a performance of Lully's Thésée he admired the hundreds of candles in crystal chandeliers, and above all the machines and rapid scene changes, which seemed to him like a world of magic. He also noticed that the audience mixed ranks and that many women attended.","approx",UFMG+"; Doha Institute review"),
E("1721-04-27","27 April 1721 (visit of 25 April)","day","penterriedter","Paris","A cordial return visit, with hints",
 "Mehmed returned the Habsburg envoy's call on 25 April. He spoke of renewing the Polish–Ottoman peace through Vienna and hinted at French offers of engineers and retired officers; Pentenriedter suspected that the envoy's real aim was intelligence.","verified",BEL),
E("1721-05-03","3 May 1721","day","wiener-diarium","Vienna","Vienna prints Paris gossip about the Turk",
 "A Paris dispatch of 21 April reported a courier from Istanbul with news of Ottoman war preparations, which the ambassador was said to think were aimed at 'a known republic' – perhaps Venice, perhaps Poland. Readers were left to guess.","verified",BEL+" (Wienerisches Diarium no. 1853)"),
E("1721-04","spring 1721","range","mehmed","Paris Observatory","Mirrors that melt lead at the Observatory",
 "Jacques Cassini showed him the Observatory's instruments, including great concave burning mirrors that melted lead before his eyes, and the moon through a telescope, which he likened to a large spongy loaf. Cassini later gave him manuscript tables of star positions, and he returned for a second visit a month later.","approx",UFMG+"; "+ARAMCO+" (exact day not established)"),
E("1721-06-07","11 Şaban 1133 AH (7 June 1721)","day","mehmed","Versailles","Five days at Versailles",
 "He arrived at Versailles with his son and stayed five days, touring the gardens, Grand Canal, Trianon and Ménagerie. He called the palace beyond compare and the Hall of Mirrors perhaps the most beautiful and charming gallery in the world, and was especially taken with Morand's automaton clock.","verified",VERS),
E("1721-06","June 1721","month","mehmed","Marly","Marly and the paradise of the infidels",
 "In the gardens of Marly, with their cascades fed by the great machine and aqueduct, he recalled the saying that the world is a prison for believers and a paradise for unbelievers – the source of the title modern editors gave his book. He compared the waterworks to the fountains of Constantinople.","approx",VERS+"; "+UFMG),
E("1721-06-18","18 June 1721","day","sutton","Paris","Word of the Malta talks",
 "Sutton reported that Dubois was treating the envoy with special courtesy in the hope of renewing the capitulations, though Mehmed had no power to negotiate. He passed on news of the Malta captives talks, learned from the Habsburg envoy.","approx",BEL+" (SP 78/169; Old/New Style not stated)"),
E("1721","1721","year","mehmed","Gobelins manufactory, Paris","Weavers working from painted models",
 "At the Gobelins he watched weavers reproduce painted cartoons in wool and silk, and remarked on the naturalism and feeling of the woven figures. The manufactory later wove a tapestry of his own procession leaving the Tuileries.","approx","Metropolitan Museum, tapestry 'The Departure of the Ottoman Embassy from the Tuileries': https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/701953 (date of visit not given)"),
E("1721","1721","year","le-dran","Paris, Hôtel des Ambassadeurs","How the ambassador dined",
 "Le Dran's memoir described the embassy's household routine, including the Ottoman custom of bringing dishes to the table one at a time rather than in French services. Such details fed Parisian curiosity, and spectators came to watch the ambassador at table.","approx",ARAMCO+"; "+UFMG),
E("1721","1721 (written c. 1739–1749)","year","saint-simon","Paris","A dignified visitor curious about books",
 "Looking back decades later, Saint-Simon remembered the ambassador as a man of presence who carried himself as if he owned the room, and as interested in books and printing, about which he already knew something. The memoir was written long after the visit.","approx",BEL+"; "+ARAMCO),
E("1721-07-18","18 July 1721","day","mehmed","Bibliothèque du Roi, Paris","Arabic manuscripts in the king's library",
 "At the royal library he was shown its many Arabic and Turkish manuscripts, including copies of the Qur'an. A Muslim visitor met his own scholarly world catalogued in a Christian king's collection.","approx",ARAMCO+"; Doha Institute review"),
E("1721-08-03","9 Şevval 1133 AH (3 August 1721)","day","mehmed","Paris","Leaving Paris after Ramadan",
 "After a farewell spectacle at Chantilly given by the Duc de Bourbon, the embassy set out for the south in early August, having observed Ramadan (late June to late July 1721) in Paris. He complained that no extra money was given for the return journey; Bonnac later called the complaint unbecoming.","approx",UFMG+"; "+ARAMCO+"; "+GH+" (sources differ between 25 July and 3 August)"),
E("1721-08-08","8 August 1721","day","penterriedter","Paris","Dubois refuses to bargain over Malta",
 "Pentenriedter's long report on the Malta captives recorded that Dubois declined to act against the Knights, calling an expedition futile and certain to anger Vienna. Mehmed retorted that French nobles served in the emperor's army.","verified",BEL),
E("1721-08","August 1721","month","mercure","Paris","Praise for an agreeable ambassador",
 "The Mercure praised the departing envoy as agreeable and of European taste. The compliment measured him by how far he resembled his hosts.","approx",GH+" (citing the annex to Veinstein's edition, p. 204)"),
E("1721-08-22","22 August 1721","day","michon","Lyon","The ambassador tours Lyon",
 "On the return journey the embassy stayed in Lyon from 20 to 28 August, and Michon's journal records the ambassador's visit to the city on the 22nd.","approx",TH1),
E("1721-09-07","7 September 1721","day","mehmed","Sète","Sailing home from Sète",
 "Back at Sète, the embassy embarked for Constantinople, returning by way of Tunis and Malta. The voyage home took about a month.","approx",ARAMCO+"; Doha Institute review"),
E("1721-10-08","16 Zilhicce 1133 AH (8 October 1721)","day","mehmed","Istanbul","Home, with a report to write",
 "Mehmed landed in Istanbul a year and a day after leaving. At the Grand Vizier's request he wrote up his journey: first a short report, then the sefaretname, presented to İbrahim Pasha and Ahmed III.","verified",BEL+" (Hijri computed, ±1 day)"),
E("1721-10-30","30 October 1721","day","dirling","Istanbul","What the envoy told the Porte",
 "Dirling heard that Dubois had sounded Mehmed about Habsburg intentions and that the envoy had replied that his instructions did not cover it. He also reported that Mehmed had urged the Porte to take up the Jacobite cause.","verified",BEL),
E("1721-11-01","21 October / 1 November 1721 (O.S./N.S.)","day","stanyan","Pera, Istanbul","Fascinated by France, wary of seeming Frankish",
 "Stanyan wrote that the embassy had cost the French treasury dearly and had turned Mehmed against Bonnac. The envoy was fascinated by France but, fearing to be branded a partisan of the 'Franks', spoke freely only among friends.","verified",BEL+" (SP 97/24)"),
E("1722-04-11","11 April 1722","day","dirling","Istanbul","A copy of the envoy's report goes to Vienna",
 "Dirling obtained a copy of the report Mehmed had written for İbrahim Pasha and sent it to the Hofkriegsrat. That the Habsburgs secured it within months shows how closely the embassy's findings were watched.","verified",BEL),
E("1722","January–May 1722","range","dirling","Istanbul","Talk of a Turkish atlas and a press",
 "Dirling reported that Mehmed's account of what Europeans did not know about the Ottoman world had led to a proposal for a Turkish atlas and a printing press, and that the envoy opposed renewing the French capitulations. Historians differ on how much the later Müteferrika press owed to the embassy.","approx",BEL+" (one of the reports of 8 Jan, 3 May or 24 May 1722)"),
]

