intro=[
 "In October 1720 an Ottoman treasurer of about fifty, Yirmisekiz Çelebi Mehmed Efendi, sailed from Istanbul on a French ship with a letter from Sultan Ahmed III for the ten-year-old Louis XV. The Peace of Passarowitz had ended a long war with the Habsburgs, and the Grand Vizier Nevşehirli Damat İbrahim Pasha, encouraged by the French ambassador Bonnac, wanted a closer look at the power that had long been the Porte's partner in the West.",
 "He arrived at the worst possible moment. Plague had broken out in Marseille, Law's financial system had just collapsed, and the foreign minister Dubois was writing that a cancelled embassy would suit him. Mehmed was held in quarantine at Toulon, shipped to Sète, and shut for forty days on the island of Maguelone, before being carried along the Canal du Midi to Toulouse and Bordeaux and up to Paris. He entered the capital on 16 March 1721, was received by the eleven-year-old king at the Tuileries on 21 March, and stayed until early August, visiting Versailles, Marly, the Observatory, the Gobelins, the opera and the royal library.",
 "His sefaretname, written after his return for the Grand Vizier, is the traveller's side of this chapter: a careful, often admiring account of canals, mirrors, machines and gardens. Many historians have linked it to the French-style pleasure gardens of the Tulip Era and, through his son Said Efendi, to the printing press İbrahim Müteferrika opened in Istanbul in 1727; others point out that the text never mentions printing and argue that the embassy's diplomatic results were slight. Both positions are given here.",
 "The hosts' record is mostly not diaries. It is administrative letters from the commander and intendant of Languedoc, who counted candles and cotton bales; dispatches from the British and Habsburg ambassadors in Paris and Istanbul, who watched the Turk as a possible ally of France; the Paris press, which saw costume and spectacle; and the memoir Saint-Simon wrote decades later. These are dated records of the visitor rather than personal journals, and are marked as such.",
 "Laid side by side, they show one traveller seen at once as a guest, a health risk, a bill, a spectacle and a spy, while he himself was looking at France as a model, a puzzle and a warning about how his own words would be read at home."
]

calendar_note=("Mehmed's sefaretname is dated by the Islamic (Hijri) lunar calendar. Equivalents here were computed with the tabular Islamic calendar and may differ by a day from local sighting: 12 Şevval 1132 = 17 August 1720; 4 Zilhicce 1132 = 7 October 1720 (departure); 1 Muharrem 1133 ≈ 2 November 1720; 21 Muharrem 1133 = 22 November 1720 (Toulon); 12 Rebiülevvel 1133, the night of the Prophet's birth and death, ≈ 11/12 January 1721; 22 Cemaziyelevvel 1133 = 21 March 1721 (audience); Ramadan 1133 ran from about 26 June to 25 July 1721, while the embassy was in Paris; 16 Zilhicce 1133 = 8 October 1721 (return). France used the Gregorian calendar, which is used for all date_iso values. British diplomats still used the Julian (Old Style) calendar, eleven days behind, and dated dispatches doubly, for example 2/13 March 1720; date_iso gives the New Style day. Mehmed's own day-counts sometimes differ by one from the French records (four days at Port-de-Bouc against three), a gap the Toulouse studies attribute to calculation.")

d=dict(id="mehmed-1720",title="The Paradise of the Infidels: An Ottoman Envoy in Regency France",short_title="An Ottoman Envoy in Regency France",
 subtitle="The embassy of Yirmisekiz Çelebi Mehmed Efendi to Louis XV — Toulon, Languedoc, Paris and Versailles, 1720–1721",span="1720–1722",
 intro=intro,calendar_note=calendar_note,eyes=eyes,entries=sorted(entries,key=lambda e:e["date_iso"]),claims=claims,convergences=convergences,ties=ties,
 threads_out=threads_out,images=images,further=further,
 vantage_key="0 = the Ottoman envoy himself; 1 = hosts and diplomats who met, escorted or provisioned him; 2 = observers in the same city working from reports; 3 = elsewhere in France; 4 = abroad (Istanbul, Vienna), news and hearsay only.")
json.dump(d,open(OUT,"w",encoding="utf-8"),ensure_ascii=False,indent=1)
ids={e["id"] for e in eyes}
for e in d["entries"]: assert e["eye"] in ids,e
for c in claims: assert c["eye"] in ids
for c in convergences: assert all(x in ids for x in c["eyes"])
for t in ties: assert t["a"] in ids and t["b"] in ids
print(len(eyes),len(entries),len(claims),len(convergences),len(ties),len(threads_out),len(images),len(further))
